 "existing_tips": [
  "'Jeune' doesn't change in feminine (already ends in -e). Most adjectives ending in -e are invariable",
  "'Jaloux' (m.) → 'jalouse' (f.) = jealous. Adjectives ending in -oux change to -ouse in feminine: dou",
  "Adjectives agree in gender: petit/petite, mince stays the same (ends in -e already). Two adjectives ",
  "'Paresseux' (m.) → 'paresseuse' (f.) = lazy. Adjectives ending in -eux → -euse in feminine.",
  "'Fier' (m.) → 'fière' (f.) = proud. The accent changes: fier/fière. 'Fier de' = proud of.",
  "'Avoir l'air + adjective' = to look/seem. J'ai l'air content = I look happy. The adjective can agree",
  "'Regarde' is the tu-form imperative. 'Regardez' for vous-form. Both can be used to draw attention.",
  "'Discret' (m.) → 'discrète' (f.) = discreet. Some -et adjectives change to -ète in feminine: secret ",
  "'Pardon' = sorry (quick apology). 'Je suis désolé(e)' = I'm sorry (deeper apology). 'Excusez-moi' = ",
  "'Je m'appelle...' = My name is... Literally 'I call myself'. This is a reflexive verb: s'appeler.",
  "Negation wraps around the verb: ne + verb + pas. 'Je ne comprends pas.' In speech, 'ne' is often dro",
  "Reflexive verbs use reflexive pronouns: je me lève, tu te lèves, il se lève, nous nous levons. The p",
  "'Porter une barbe' = to wear/have a beard. French uses 'porter' (to wear) for facial hair.",
  "70 = soixante-dix (60+10), 80 = quatre-vingts (4×20), 90 = quatre-vingt-dix (4×20+10). Unique to Fre",
  "'S'il vous plaît' (formal/plural) vs 's'il te plaît' (informal). Literally 'if it pleases you'. Esse",
  "'Enchanté(e)' literally means 'enchanted'. It agrees with the speaker's gender: enchanté (m.), encha",
  "Numbers 21-69 follow a pattern: vingt et un (21), trente-deux (32). After 70, French uses base-20: s",
  "'Écoute' is the informal imperative (tu form) of 'écouter'. Used as an interjection to get attention",
  "'Je vous en prie' is the most formal way to say 'you're welcome'. More casual: 'de rien' or 'pas de ",
  "'Mignon' (m.) → 'mignonne' (f.) = cute. Adjectives ending in -on double the 'n' in feminine: bon → b"
